{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00153 - MONDO_5520",
  "verbalization": "Ergocalciferol is implicated in the pathogenesis of rickets through a series of interconnected pathways that involve hypercalcemia and vitamin D metabolism. The evidence suggests that Ergocalciferol is linked to hypercalcemia, which in turn is associated with calcifediol and cholecalciferol, both of which are known to contribute to rickets. This indicates that Ergocalciferol may act as a precursor or mediator in the development of rickets by influencing calcium homeostasis and vitamin D signaling. The presence of multiple indirect pathways—through hypercalcemia and vitamin deficiency—suggests a broader role in the disease process, potentially involving systemic effects on calcium and vitamin D levels. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of vitamin D metabolism in rickets, with Ergocalciferol possibly serving as a key intermediate in the cascade leading to rickets.",
